@node Compatibility
@chapter Compatibility with the original xwpe

@cindex compatibility
@cindex differences from xwpe 1.4

This chapter lists what xwpe now does differently from the xwpe of the
1990s, where a user of the original would notice it.  Each entry says
what the original did, what xwpe does now, and why.

@section While the debugger answers at a stop

@table @asis
@item Ctrl-C while the debugger answers
@cindex Ctrl-C, while gdb answers
The original asked, in a box, whether to quit the debugger when
@kbd{Ctrl-C} was typed while it read the debugger's answer.  Under gdb,
pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, @kbd{Ctrl-C} now stops waiting for the
answer, as @key{Esc}
does, and the status row says which command the debugger did not answer;
Debug > Quit ends the session.  The box is gone: the editor keeps the
keyboard while the debugger answers, at a stop, while the debugger starts
or when a run or a step goes out, so there is no moment where only a box
could get you out.

@item Keys typed while the debugger answers
@cindex keys, while gdb answers
The original passed every key typed while it read the debugger's answer
on to the debugger.  Under gdb, pdb, jdb, a68g, sdb, dbx and xdb, keys
typed then are the editor's: text
goes into the file, menus open, and a debugger command waits for the
answer or is refused with the waiting line.  A key that edits acts at
once, so it may act before the step typed just before it has stopped.
Keys that reached the debugger behind the editor's back were how an
answer could be lost or a letter typed into the source.
@end table

@section When the editor ends during a debug session

@table @asis
@item The debugger at the editor's end
@cindex debugger, at the editor's end
The original sent the debugger signal 7 when it ended during a session,
which on Linux is SIGBUS: the debugger died with a core file and could
leave its program running.  xwpe now ends it as Debug > Quit does: told
to quit at its prompt, or sent SIGTERM while its program runs, and
killed only if it is still there two seconds later, so the debugger ends
its program and leaves no core file.
@end table

@section Where the program under the debugger writes

@table @asis
@item The program's terminal
@cindex debugger, terminal of the program
The original terminal editor refused a session unless its own input was
a terminal, and sent the program's output there, over the editor's
screen.  xwpe gives the program a
pseudoterminal of its own and reads its output through it, so a session
starts even when the editor has no terminal, as when it is started from a
desktop launcher.  The editor's terminal is used only when no
pseudoterminal could be opened.
@end table

@section Keys

@table @asis
@item F11 and F12 under X
@cindex F11, F12
The original read the key symbols of @key{F11} and @key{F12} as the Sun
keyboard's Stop and Again keys, which they also are, so a PC keyboard's
@key{F11} quit the editor and @key{F12} redid.  They now do nothing, as
they always did in a terminal: one key gives one action on every display,
and quitting on a key no menu names surprised users.

@item Alt-Delete in a terminal
@cindex Alt-Delete, in a terminal
The original read Esc then Delete in a terminal as Shift-Delete and cut
the marked block.  It is now @kbd{Alt-Delete} on every display, which
copies the block (to the X selection under X, to the buffer in a
terminal) and leaves it in the file.
@end table

@section What arrives while a box is open

@table @asis
@item Output, stops and answers under a dialog or a menu
@cindex dialog, output while open
The original did nothing in the background: while a build ran, the
debugger started or the program ran, the editor waited, so no menu or
dialog could be open when output or a stop arrived.  xwpe keeps the
keyboard meanwhile, so a box can be open when they arrive; what arrives
then is kept and drawn when the box closes (@pxref{Interface tour}).  A
box drawn over is a box you can no longer read.
@end table

@section Short commands

@table @asis
@item Print, manual pages and compressed help files
@cindex print command, time limit
@cindex man, time limit
The original ran the print command, @command{man} and @command{gunzip}
through the shell and waited for them with no limit, the editor frozen
meanwhile.  Each now has a time limit (a minute to print, 30 seconds for
a page or an unpack), the window is drawn again while it runs, and one
that takes longer than a second is named on the status row.  A command
that hung used to leave no way out but killing the editor.
@end table
